course: dictionary
lesson: 5850
title: Names and borrowed words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are names. A name tells you who someone is, and it starts with a capital letter, like Malik. «Malik's aunt already has three kids, so Malik shares a bunk bed with his cousin Dre.» Here Malik is a boy's name, so it is a proper noun. You do not look for its meaning the way you look for a word like dog.
explain: Some words come into English from other languages, and Malik is one of them. In Arabic, Urdu and some other languages, «malik» means king or owner. English does not use it that way, so in an English sentence it is almost always a name. Check the sentence: is it talking about a person, or about a king?
explain: Context still helps. «We're like brothers now,» Malik says, and they fight like brothers too. The words brothers and fight tell you that Malik is a person who is close to someone. A name does not have a meaning you can look up in the same way as other words. You learn who a name belongs to from the story.
fact: Malik is a name used in many Muslim, Arab and South Asian families.
fact: As a name, Malik is written with a capital letter, as in Mr Malik or Malik's family.
fact: In Arabic and Urdu, «malik» means king or owner, but in English it is only a name or a borrowed word.
fact: In RE lessons about Islam, «Al-Malik» (the King) is one of the names Muslims use for God.
fact: A proper noun is the name of a particular person, place or thing, and it always starts with a capital letter.
exercise: «Malik's aunt already has three kids, so Malik shares a bunk bed with his cousin Dre.» What kind of word is Malik here? | A word for a cousin | A type of bed | A place in a city | *A name for a person
exercise: «We're like brothers now,» Malik says. What does this tell you about Malik? | He is a horse | He is a building | *He is a person who is close to someone | He is a king
exercise: Which fact about «malik» is true? | In English it means a type of bread | It is always written in lower case | It is a French word for a river | *In Arabic and Urdu it can mean king or owner
test: «In Philadelphia, Malik, 16, has friends round, and Dante, 10, tries to hang with them.» Is Malik a name or a word meaning king? | *A name | A place in Philadelphia | A word meaning owner | A word meaning king
test: Why do we write Malik with a capital letter when it is a name? | Capital letters make words sound louder | *Names are proper nouns and start with a capital letter | Every word in English starts with a capital | Only kings have capital letters
exam: Ella says «malik» means a king in English stories. Is she right, and how do you check? | Yes, it always means king in every English book | *No, in English it is a name; check the sentence to see if it talks about a person | Yes, because it has a capital letter, and that proves it | No, it is a fruit, so it cannot be a name
